From 362d6a21d4a4c07751c129125561f05646f8c219 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 21:03:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 10: compute closure WPD type ids from a text
 that writes each record body once

With closure devirtualization (`-O aggressive`), `closureWpdTypeId`
(ClosureWpd.h) names a closure's return type by the BLAKE3 digest of its
printed form, at every vtable (`stampClosureWpdTypeIds`) and at every
indirect call, clone and drop site (`emitClosureWpdTest`, both in
BasicOpsLowering). `RecordType::print` writes a named record's body at
each occurrence and stops only at a record already on its print stack,
so a record reached along k paths is written k times: for
`D(i) = struct(D(i-1), D(i-1))` the text of D(K) holds 2^K copies of
D0. rrc took 24 s and 250 MB for K = 20 and 89 s and 730 MB for K = 22
(0.4 s with --no-closure-wpd), and two lean2rr programs with polymorphic
recursion through monad transformers ran out of 16 GB.

Add `printTypeWithRecordBodiesOnce` (ReussirTypes.h): the usual printer,
except that under it `RecordType::print` writes a named record's body
only at its first occurrence and later occurrences as `<kind "name">`,
the form it already uses for a self-reference. The text still determines
the type (a named record is unique by name in a context and its body is
written once), so equal return types still get equal ids in any module
and distinct ones distinct ids, and each named record costs its body
once instead of once per path. `closureWpdTypeId` digests that text
(described in ClosureWpd.h and docs/design/closure-wpd.md). Only named
records are shortened: an unnamed composite type that repeats
along many paths (a closure type `T(i+1) = (T(i)) -> T(i)`) is still
written in full each time, as before (rrc is exponential on such types
without closure devirtualization too).

Types in which no named record repeats print exactly as before, so their
ids are unchanged; where one repeats, the id changes but the grouping of
vtables and call sites into families does not, and neither does the
generated code: the object files of the bug's repro (K = 12) are
identical, and the LLVM IR of Reussir's frontend tests is identical
after renaming the ids in order of appearance.

Now K = 22 builds in 0.4 s, with or without closure devirtualization.

Found by lean2rr (docs/reussir-bugs.md, bug 10); lean2rr passes
--no-closure-wpd to avoid it.
Test: tests/integration/frontend/closure_wpd_nested_records (a 22-level
tower: the two D22 closures share an id, the D21 closure has another;
23 s and 690 MB before).
